    "original_a": "Bayes theorem: P(COVID | positive) = P(positive | COVID) × P(COVID) / P(positive). Given: sensitivity (true positive rate) = 95%, specificity (true negative rate) = 90%, prevalence = 2%. Step 1-P(positive | COVID) = 0.95 (sensitivity). P(positive | no COVID) = 1 - specificity = 0.10 (false positive rate). Step 2-P(COVID) = 0.02, P(no COVID) = 0.98. Step 3-P(positive) = P(positive | COVID) × P(COVID) + P(positive | no COVID) × P(no COVID) = 0.95 × 0.02 + 0.10 × 0.98 = 0.019 + 0.098 = 0.117. Step 4-P(COVID | positive) = 0.95 × 0.02 / 0.117 = 0.019 / 0.117 approximately 0.162 (about 16.2%). Shocking result: test seems 95% accurate but positive result only means 16% chance of actual COVID! Reason: disease rare (2%), false positives common (10% of 98% = 10%). Base rate dominates. Real-world implication: massive over-testing, unnecessary panic, medical resource strain. Solution: test twice (positive + positive → ~97% confidence), or test high-risk populations (10% prevalence → 51% posterior probability, much better). Medical screening requires balancing sensitivity (catch disease) vs specificity (avoid false alarms) and population prevalence. Classic example of base rate fallacy-ignoring population prevalence leads to wrong decisions."
